<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Janus of Cyprus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1375–<a href="/years/1432">1432</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lusignan">House of Lusignan</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Janus was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> from 1398 to 1432. Born while his father was a prisoner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, he tried without success to recover Famagusta from the Genoese. Cypriot raids provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barsbay">Barsbay</a>, whose army invaded in 1426, defeated Janus at Khirokitia and took him prisoner to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. He was freed for a large ransom, and Cyprus became tributary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/janus-of-cyprus.jpg" alt="Janus of Cyprus" width="302" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rama · CC BY-SA 3.0 cz · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Janus_of_Cyprus_CharlotteVendome.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Janus was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, where his father, James I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, was held captive. His mother, Helvis of Brunswick-Grubenhagen, named him after the god Janus, the founder of Genoa according to mythological tradition. When his father was elected king, he agreed with the Genoese on 2 February 1383 to be released, and they were given new commercial privileges, but they demanded that Janus stay in their city as a hostage. According to the Cypriot historian Leontios Makhairas, James ordered a special tax requiring Cypriots to buy salt, to raise the money to release his son, who was freed in October 1392, aged 18.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death of his father on 9 September 1398 Janus inherited the throne and was crowned in Saint Sophia Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a> on 11 November 1398. In 1402 he conspired with a priest and with Peter Makhairas to retake Famagusta from the Genoese, but the plan was betrayed and 28 conspirators were executed. He then besieged Famagusta for three years without success, because the city could be supplied from the sea, and the siege ended in 1406. Epidemics and locusts struck the island from 1408, and a new epidemic in 1419-1420 probably caused the death of his second wife, Charlotte de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, on 15 January 1422.</p><p>Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> raided from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and Janus could not stop them, which gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barsbay">Barsbay</a> reason to retaliate. After smaller attacks, a large army of over 3,000 men, with 180 ships, landed near Avdimou in summer 1426. Janus asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for help in vain. In the battle that followed, on 7 July 1426, he was captured, and the Mamluks went on to pillage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larnaca">Larnaca</a> again and the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>, taking much loot and many captives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Janus was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> for ten months, during which his brother Hugh of Lusignan, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>, governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. He was taken before the sultan in chains on a donkey and forced to kneel nine times. Europeans raised the ransom, and Cyprus also had to pay the sultan an annual tax, which continued to be paid even after the end of Frankish rule in Cyprus. During his captivity serfs revolted under a man named Alexis, who was declared king at Lefkoniko, and the rebellion was put down after ten months. Janus arrived in Paphos from Cairo on 12 May 1427. He died on 29 June 1432 and left nine children.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Janus, King of Cyprus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Genoa seized Famagusta in 1373, and a Mamluk invasion in 1426 captured King <b>Janus</b> and made the kingdom tributary to Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cyprus">Kingdom of Cyprus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1412" aria-label="Genoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Janus of Cyprus"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5544 3760h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5544 3760h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1412">What was here in 1412? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
